  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper presents a circuit-guided defense and makes a claim about refusal-direction propagation, but its interventions target circuit-identified attention heads rather than the difference-in-means refusal direction. It does not directly test any listed claim version.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports head ablation, channel-wise head scaling, preventative fine-tuning, and jailbreak/capability outcomes. These are results about a distinct head-and-circuit method, not tests of the claimed single refusal direction. Its assertion that adversarial suffixes suppress propagation of a refusal-mediating direction is not accompanied by a direct measurement of that direction. Cited findings from other work are not extracted.",
    "test_reason": "The study does not test the refusal direction. Its interventions zero, scale, or fine-tune around circuit-identified attention heads. It never computes, ablates, adds, or measures a difference-in-means refusal direction. The abstract asserts that adversarial suffixes suppress propagation of the refusal-mediating direction, but no result measures that direction. The link to V5 (harm judged separately from refusal) is interpretation: §6.1 cites Zhao et al. 2025, while the probes measure tense, not harmfulness or refusal. The study does not rely on the direction as a tool, so T4 is wrong; it discusses and cites the finding (T6).",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right. The authors conclude that circuit-identified tense-vulnerable heads, rescaled by channel-wise vectors and followed by preventative fine-tuning, cut targeted jailbreak ASR with a Pareto-optimal safety-utility balance. They also assert, without measuring it, that adversarial suffixes suppress propagation of the refusal-mediating direction. They interpret tense jailbreaks as the refusal mechanism being bypassed rather than harm going unrecognised, citing Zhao et al. 2025.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The method identifies attack-specific attention-head circuits, learns channel-wise scaling vectors for those heads, and fine-tunes while the vectors are applied before detaching them. It does not find or project out a harmful-minus-harmless residual-stream direction, or add that direction. This is a mechanistic defense paper, not a replication of the original direction method.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported outcomes vary by model and attack. The paper also describes circuit construction choices including five sampled refusal prompts and thresholds from 0.1 to 0.03; the basic system message is specified as “You are a helpful AI assistant.”",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Zeroing circuit-identified tense-vulnerable heads cuts past-tense ASR by 4–13 points; random heads cut it by about 1–2. Channel-wise scaling plus preventative fine-tuning (ASGuard) brings ASR to 8–19% while keeping MMLU, but raises OR-Bench-Hard over-refusal in all four models.",
        "proposed_claim": "Attack-specific jailbreak vulnerability is localised in a small set of attention heads, and targeted head scaling plus fine-tuning reduces it at some over-refusal cost.",
